.Dd November 17, 2026
.Dt TALK 1
.Os RavenBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm talk
.Nd talk to another user
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar address
.Op Ar terminal
.Nm
.Fl l
.Ar address
.Op Ar terminal
.Nm
.Fl -loopback
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility establishes a two-way, screen-oriented session with another local user.
The
.Ar address
operand has the form
.Sm off
.Ar user Op @ Ar host .
.Sm on
The optional
.Ar terminal
operand selects one of the recipient's terminals.
.Pp
A listener runs
.Nm
.Fl l
with its own user name.
It registers the terminal in the
.Ql ttyd
logged-in terminal registry and waits for an invitation.
A caller opens its own registry entry, looks the recipient up in
.Ql ttyd
and connects to the complementary end of the recipient's pty pair.
The registry record carries the tty path and the writable state; a terminal
with messages disabled is refused.
.Pp
The caller sends an invitation with the
.Dq "Message from" ,
.Dq "connection requested by"
and
.Dq "respond with"
lines.
The recipient accepts or declines.
Once active, both parties can type simultaneously, and their text is shown in separate regions
of the screen.
The invitation and session are carried by the bounded
.Dq RVTALK/1
protocol; pty pairs other than pair 0 provide independent transports.
.Pp
The following controls are handled:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Ctrl-C
Send
.Dv BYE
and terminate the local session with status 0.
.It Ctrl-D
End the local input and notify the peer.
.It Ctrl-L
Refresh the local split screen.
.It Ctrl-G
Alert the recipient's terminal.
.El
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl l
Listen for an invitation instead of sending one.
The
.Ar address
operand names the local user to register.
This is a local extension; POSIX defines no
.Nm
options.
.It Fl -loopback
Run both ends of the protocol in one process and print a readable transcript.
This is a local diagnostic for the bounded protocol, not a peer session.
.El
.Sh CURRENT LIMITATION
Network
.Ar host
addresses other than
.Ql localhost
are not implemented; there is no
.Nm talkd
to carry an invitation between machines.
The image has one
.Xr consd 8
console UI, so two local peers share that console; the pty transports are
independent.
There is no
.Ql mesg
command-line toggle and no extended
.Ql stty iexten
control handling.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
A declined invitation, unsupported network address, unreachable recipient,
disabled messages, missing pty transport or protocol failure exits with status
1; a usage error exits with status 2.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr write 1 ,
.Xr consd 8
.Sh STANDARDS
RavenBSD implements the documented POSIX
.Nm
invitation, split-screen, forwarding, EOF and termination model with the
current-limitation noted above.
